ZTEXT001000016ed34cd0ebd0000000
Ezero files, volumes and process arguments

These functions call original Zero platform adapters. They do not launch a
shell or execute filenames, file contents or argument text as code.

Expressions
  host()                    1 Linux, 2 Windows, 0 Zero OS
  arguments()               one-based list of native user arguments
  read(path)                read complete text; readfile is the same operation
  save(path | text)         create or replace text; return UTF-8 byte count
  writefile(path | text)    exclusively create new text; return byte count
  create(path)              create empty file; preserve existing file; return 1
  del(path)                 delete an existing regular file; return 1
  rm(path)                  alias for del
  watch(root)               one-based list of regular filenames in that root
  search(patternpath)       one-based list of matching regular filenames
  go(root)                  change the current root/directory; return 1
  disks()                   one-based list of available volume roots
  fileexists(path)          1 for a readable regular file, otherwise 0

Lists contain filenames, not full paths, and have no guaranteed order. search
matches the final component: * means any number of Unicode scalars, ? means one scalar;
without wildcards the name must match exactly. Matching is case-sensitive.
There is no recursive traversal, environment expansion or home expansion.
watch(.) uses the current directory. An unmatched search returns an empty list.
A failed listing is an error, not an empty result. Results must fit 65536 bytes
including internal terminators and at most 2048 names. UTF-8 names are validated
before becoming managed text; invalid names cause a source-line error.

File contents and the system container
read/readfile and save/writefile work with raw UTF-8 file contents. A filename
has no implicit format: save(notes | Hello) stores the five bytes Hello.
It does not produce a ZTEXT001 document or decode a container automatically.
Use the original zero-text make/read/verify commands for the checked ZTEXT001
format. Ezero currently has no high-level Ztext encoder/decoder builtin.

Native paths and data
Linux and Windows use their native filesystem paths. Paths are nonempty strict
UTF-8 text without NUL, at most 4095 bytes; platforms may impose stricter limits.
Files contain at most 65536 bytes of strict UTF-8 without NUL. Newlines and
Unicode are preserved. size(read(path)) counts Unicode scalars; save/writefile
return bytes. Missing, unreadable, nonregular and final-symlink file paths fail.
No parent directories are created automatically. Native go changes this
process's working directory. Linux disks() currently returns [/], not a list
of physical devices or mounted filesystems. Windows returns available drive
roots; these are volume roots, not a physical-disk inventory.

save validates text and size before opening the destination, then writes,
synchronizes and closes it. It deliberately replaces existing content.
A failed native save can leave a truncated or partially written destination;
it is not an atomic replacement or rollback operation.

Native writefile never replaces an existing file, directory or link. It creates
an exclusive sibling temporary file, handles partial writes, closes it and
publishes with same-filesystem NOREPLACE rename. A failed publish preserves the
existing destination and attempts to remove its own staging file. Up to 64
exclusive staging names are tried; pre-existing staging names are preserved.
The staging basename uses 31 ASCII bytes; the parent prefix must fit 4064 bytes.
This is atomic publication, not a crash-durability guarantee. An interrupted
process can leave its unpublished staging file behind.

create preserves an existing regular file's contents. del errors if the file
is missing. fileexists cannot distinguish absent from inaccessible; it is not
a reservation or proof that a later operation will succeed. The actual
operation enforces its own rules. Paths and external state may change between
calls. Successful create/del/go return the Ezero number 1; errors stop the
program with the Ezero source line and exit 65.

Zero OS profile
The same functions use the kernel's file service in a guest; host()==0 does
not disable them. Zero OS uses roots such as A:| and flat filenames up to 31
ASCII bytes. Bare paths such as A:|note are accepted directly by Ezero. The
current writable ZeroFS profile stores at most 400 printable ASCII bytes per
file. Native Unicode/newline text support does not expand that disk format.
Unsupported paths, content, read-only volumes, unavailable service and failed
operations report a source-line error. The native filesystem adapter's
publication rules do not imply identical crash behavior on a ZeroFS disk.
Other mounted formats keep the kernel's existing support limits. Formatting is
a separate explicit administrative terminal operation, not part of this API.

Process arguments
arguments() is available on native Linux/Windows only. It returns up to 63
items excluding the executable name, preserving empty arguments and Unicode.
Each item is at most 4095 UTF-8 bytes and the total at most 65536 bytes. Windows
currently bounds the complete converted command line to 4095 UTF-8 bytes.
arguments()[1] is the first item; index 0 errors. Zero OS reports that process
arguments are unavailable; optional native argument handling can use host().

Examples
  create(notes) => created
  save(notes | Hello from Ezero) => bytes
  read(notes) => show
  search("note*") => each name [ name => show ]
  del(notes) => removed

  go(A:|) => selected
  create(A:|note) => created
  save(A:|note | Three bananas) => bytes
  watch(A:|) => show

Quotes are optional for ordinary text and Zero paths. Wildcards and native
paths containing syntax characters use optional quotes or a text binding.
